# Caddyfile — TLS termination and names for every web UI on this box.
#
# Result: https://<service>.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}, with a real Let's Encrypt
# certificate that every browser trusts, on a LAN-only box behind CGNAT.
#
# HOW THAT IS POSSIBLE, since none of this is reachable from the internet:
#   * DOMAIN and *.DOMAIN are PUBLIC DNS records that resolve to the Pi's
#     LAN address — a private address. Public name,
#     private destination. Nothing outside the house can use it; DNS is just a
#     directory, publishing a name costs nothing and exposes nothing.
#   * The certificate is issued over DNS-01, which proves control of the NAME by
#     writing a TXT record, not control of a reachable HTTP server. So Let's
#     Encrypt never connects to this Pi. See caddy/Dockerfile for why HTTP-01
#     can never work here.
#
# ONE CERTIFICATE, ONE CHALLENGE — this is the load-bearing design decision.
# Every service is a `handle` inside the single `*.DOMAIN` block below rather
# than its own site block, because DuckDNS stores exactly ONE TXT record per
# account. Split these into seven site blocks and Caddy orders seven separate
# certificates, whose DNS-01 challenges each overwrite the previous one's TXT
# record — they fail, retry, and can burn through Let's Encrypt's rate limit
# (5 failed validations per hour). One wildcard name = one TXT write = no race.
# The apex is deliberately NOT in this certificate for the same reason.
#
# ⚠ NOTHING HERE SHOULD EVER BE PORT-FORWARDED ON THE ROUTER. Valid TLS does
# not make Arcane (control of every container on the box), Pi-hole (DNS for the
# whole house) or the dashboard (an index of every service here) safe to expose.
# The point of this file is trusted certificates on the LAN, not remote access.
# Under CGNAT a forward would not work anyway — do not treat that as the reason.
{
	# No ACME account email, at the owner's choice (2026-08-26). Consequence:
	# Let's Encrypt has no way to warn about a certificate that has stopped
	# renewing, so disk-guard.sh checks the expiry locally instead and trips
	# `systemctl --failed`, which is this box's entire alerting system.
	# To turn LE's own warnings back on, add:  email you@example.com
	#
	# The admin API is left at its default (127.0.0.1:2019, inside the
	# container's own namespace — not reachable from the LAN). It is what the
	# healthcheck below uses, and what `docker exec caddy caddy reload` needs.
	# Do not add `admin off` without changing both.
}

# Applied to the wildcard site. Split out only so the reasoning has somewhere to
# live without burying the routing table underneath it.
(duckdns_tls) {
	tls {
		dns duckdns {env.DUCKDNS_TOKEN}

		# ⚠ THIS LINE IS NOT OPTIONAL ON THIS PARTICULAR BOX.
		# After writing the TXT record, Caddy polls DNS to confirm it has
		# propagated. Left alone it would use the container's resolver, which
		# is Docker's, which forwards to the host, which is PI-HOLE — running
		# on this same Pi. Pi-hole will have already cached the OLD (empty)
		# TXT answer, and serves that cached negative for its full TTL. Caddy
		# then waits for a record it has already set, times out, and issuance
		# fails in a way that looks like DuckDNS being broken. Asking public
		# resolvers directly sidesteps our own cache.
		resolvers 1.1.1.1 9.9.9.9

		# VERIFIED 2026-08-26, because the opposite would be catastrophic and
		# silent: a DuckDNS /update call WITHOUT an `ip=` parameter sets the A
		# record to the CALLER's address. If the plugin's TXT writes did that,
		# every renewal would repoint the domain at this house's
		# public CGNAT address (which also rotates) and every
		# name here would break at once, roughly every 60 days, for no visible
		# reason. Tested by hand with a canary TXT: the A record stayed
		# on the LAN address. A txt-only update does NOT touch the A record.
		#
		# Corollary: never run the stock DuckDNS updater cron on this network.
		# It has no ip= either, and it WOULD do exactly that damage.

		# DuckDNS applies TXT updates in seconds, but the propagation check is
		# the difference between "renewed quietly at 3am" and "failed once an
		# hour for a week", so it is worth being patient here.
		propagation_delay 30s
		propagation_timeout 5m
	}
}

# ============================================================ admin allowlist ==
# The per-vhost half of the two-tier scheme in config/nftables.conf. The
# firewall cannot do this part: every name below arrives on the same TCP :443,
# so at layer 4 docker.DOMAIN and home.DOMAIN are indistinguishable. Only Caddy
# knows which vhost was asked for, so the vhost-level allowlist has to live
# here — driven by the SAME ADMIN_SOURCES value from .env, so the two halves
# cannot drift.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS NOT AUTHENTICATION. It is a reachability tier: it keeps a
# compromised TV or IoT bulb on the LAN away from the admin UIs entirely,
# rather than leaving it to guess their passwords. Every service behind it
# keeps its own login; do not remove one because this exists.
#
# ⚠ CADDY MUST SEE THE REAL CLIENT ADDRESS FOR THIS TO MEAN ANYTHING. It does:
# Docker publishes :443 with DNAT, which rewrites the destination and leaves
# the source alone, so remote_ip sees the LAN client. Two consequences worth
# knowing: a request from the Pi ITSELF arrives from the bridge gateway
# (172.18.0.1), and if you ever put another reverse proxy in front of this one,
# remote_ip becomes that proxy's address and this whole snippet silently
# allows everything. Do not chain proxies without also configuring trusted_proxies.
#
# ⚠ 100.64.0.0/10 (the tailnet) is included below because at layer 7 there is
# no interface to match on — a Tailscale peer is just an address. That range is
# ALSO the ISP's CGNAT space, which looks alarming, but nothing from the
# carrier side can reach :443 in the first place: nftables drops every
# non-private source at input and admits the tailnet by interface name only.
# The interface check happens before Caddy ever sees the packet.
(adminonly) {
	@notadmin not remote_ip {$ADMIN_SOURCES} 100.64.0.0/10
	respond @notadmin "403 — this service is restricted to admin sources. See ADMIN_SOURCES in .env." 403
}

# Shared proxy hygiene. Caddy already sets X-Forwarded-For / -Proto / -Host and
# passes the original Host through, which is all any of these apps need — but
# WebSocket upgrades (Homebridge's log stream, Arcane's live container view)
# only survive because Caddy proxies hop-by-hop headers correctly by default.
# Do not "helpfully" add header_up rules; they are what break it.

# ============================================================ HTTPS services ==
*.{$CADDY_DOMAIN} {
	import duckdns_tls

	# ------------------------------------------------------------ dashboard --
	# NOT behind (adminonly), by design: the dashboard and the pastebin are
	# household services and every device in the house is meant to open them.
	# The dashboard only LINKS to the admin UIs — an unauthorised source that
	# follows one of those links gets the 403 at the far end, which is the
	# intended experience rather than a bug to fix by widening the allowlist.
	@home host home.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @home {
		reverse_proxy starbase80:4173
	}

	# -------------------------------------------------------------- Pi-hole --
	# host.docker.internal, not a container name: Pi-hole runs with
	# network_mode: host (it must own :53 on the LAN address), so it is not on
	# the compose bridge network and has no DNS name there. The
	# extra_hosts/host-gateway entry in docker-compose.yml is what makes this
	# resolve to the bridge gateway (172.18.0.1). The nftables ruleset accepts
	# it because 172.16/12 is RFC1918, which is already allowed wholesale.
	#
	# ⚠ EVERY ADMIN HANDLE FROM HERE ON USES `route { import adminonly; ... }`.
	# The `route` is not decoration. Outside one, Caddy sorts directives by its
	# own table rather than by the order written — `respond` does currently sort
	# ahead of `reverse_proxy` (verified against Caddy 2.11.4), so the guard
	# would still fire, but `redir` sorts ahead of BOTH and an unauthorised
	# client would collect the 302 before the 403. `route` pins the written
	# order, which makes the guard's position a property of this file rather
	# than of Caddy's version.
	@pihole host pihole.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @pihole {
		route {
			import adminonly
			# Pi-hole v6 serves nothing useful at /, so save the click.
			redir / /admin/ 302
			reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8080
		}
	}

	# ---------------------------------------------------------- Filebrowser --
	@files host files.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @files {
		route {
			import adminonly
			reverse_proxy filebrowser:80
		}
	}

	# ------------------------------------------------------------- MicroBin --
	# ⚠ HTTPS CHANGES NOTHING ABOUT MICROBIN'S EXPOSURE. /upload/<id> and
	# /raw/<id> still return 200 with full content and no credentials — that is
	# upstream behaviour and is not configurable. Encrypting the transport does
	# not add an authorisation check. Still do not paste secrets here.
	@paste host paste.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @paste {
		reverse_proxy microbin:8080
	}

	# --------------------------------------------------------- Transmission --
	# Works unmodified because BOTH of Transmission's whitelists are off
	# (verified 2026-08-26: rpc-host-whitelist-enabled false,
	# rpc-whitelist-enabled false), so neither the rewritten Host nor the
	# proxy's 172.18.x source address is rejected. If either is ever turned
	# back on, this breaks with "403: Unrecognized Host header" or "403:
	# Unauthorized IP Address" — the fix is `header_up Host localhost` and
	# adding 172.18.* to rpc-whitelist, NOT disabling the RPC password.
	@torrents host torrents.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @torrents {
		route {
			import adminonly
			redir / /transmission/web/ 302
			reverse_proxy transmission:9091
		}
	}

	# --------------------------------------------------------------- Arcane --
	# ⚠ THE MOST DANGEROUS NAME IN THIS FILE. Arcane reaches the Docker API
	# through socket-proxy-rw, which is deliberately narrowed (no EXEC, see
	# docker-compose.yml) — but it can still start, stop and recreate every
	# container, and a container can be given any host mount. Whoever logs in
	# can take DNS down for the house or read .env. LAN only, admin only.
	@docker host docker.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @docker {
		route {
			import adminonly
			reverse_proxy arcane:3552
		}
	}

	# ----------------------------------------------------------- Homebridge --
	# host networking again, for the same reason as Pi-hole: mDNS/Bonjour does
	# not survive bridge networking, and HomeKit is entirely mDNS.
	@homebridge host homebridge.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @homebridge {
		route {
			import adminonly
			reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8581
		}
	}

	# --------------------------------------------------------------- Dozzle --
	# ⚠ Container logs contain more than they look like they do — environment
	# dumps, tokens in error messages, file paths. Dozzle has its own login
	# (simple auth, appdata/dozzle/users.yml); do not remove it, and never
	# forward 8085. Treat a Dozzle session as equivalent to reading .env.
	@logs host logs.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @logs {
		route {
			import adminonly
			reverse_proxy dozzle:8080
		}
	}

	# --------------------------------------------------------------- Beszel --
	# The hub only. The agent talks to it over the compose network / host, not
	# through Caddy, so there is nothing to proxy for it here.
	@metrics host metrics.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}
	handle @metrics {
		route {
			import adminonly
			reverse_proxy beszel:8090
		}
	}

	# Anything else under the wildcard — a typo, or a service that was removed.
	# An explicit answer beats a confusing default page.
	handle {
		respond "No service by that name on this Pi. Try https://home.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}" 404
	}
}

# =============================================================== HTTP names ===
# Caddy owns :80 now, so the pre-existing ways into the dashboard have to keep
# working or every bookmark in the house breaks at once. These three names
# cannot have certificates — a private IP and the reserved .internal / .local
# TLDs are not things any public CA will ever sign — so they stay plain HTTP,
# and are the fallback if TLS or DuckDNS is ever broken.
# ⚠ {$VAR} is Caddy's own PARSE-TIME environment substitution, not shell
# syntax. These three names come from LAN_IP / LOCAL_DNS_NAME / LOCAL_HOSTNAME,
# which docker-compose.yml passes into this container from .env.
http://{$LAN_IP}, http://{$LOCAL_DNS_NAME}, http://{$LOCAL_HOSTNAME}.local {
	reverse_proxy starbase80:4173
}

# The apex is not in the wildcard certificate (see the header), so it is served
# over HTTP only and just points at the dashboard.
http://{$CADDY_DOMAIN} {
	redir https://home.{$CADDY_DOMAIN}{uri} permanent
}

# Every other http:// name under the domain redirects to itself over HTTPS.
# Caddy would normally generate these redirects automatically, but it does not
# do so for a wildcard site, so it is spelled out.
http://*.{$CADDY_DOMAIN} {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
